 \node[above] at (2.7,-.2){Specified causal effect};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{In the constructed Gaussian example, measurement error can reverse
the sign of an adjusted regression coefficient. The causal effect is fixed at
$\tau=-1/5$; the solid curve is $-1/5+s/(1+2s)$. This is an exact population
calculation, not observed performance of a language model.}
\label{reading:measurement-error}
\end{figure}

The appropriate comparison is consequently broader than extraction accuracy.
Simple dictionaries, supervised extraction, human coding and language models
should use the same permitted document dates and the same causal target.
The evaluation should ask whether their measurement errors change the estimated
effect or the chosen action. It should also test cases in which the notes were
written after treatment or only after a complaint. Those cases alter the
causal role of the text, not merely its predictive quality.
\citep{exec13_census_text_review_arxiv}

This example concerns the validity of the measurement used for adjustment.
The task comparisons in Chapter~\ref{rewrite:assistance-tasks} separately
address formulation, discovery, formal reasoning, execution and reporting.
They distinguish supplied-structure benchmarks from elicitation of a new
bank mechanism. \citep{v12_la_2312_04350,v12_la_2606_23608}

\part{Choosing methods for discovery and identification}
\label{reading:part-methods}
Software is useful when its methods answer the economic question under
credible assumptions. The comparisons begin with the relationships that a
method can discover and the effects it can identify. They distinguish a supplied
causal model from one learned from data and compare alternatives across languages.

\section{Reusing R bnlearn without losing graph, intervention or sampling meaning}
\label{sec:exec24-bnlearn-reuse}
A banking team may want one workflow that learns a dependency graph, fits local distributions, simulates an intervention and reports a customer-level counterfactual. These are different inferential tasks even when a package exposes them through neighboring functions. R bnlearn provides a useful concrete case for the proposed library: preserve the package's graph and fitted-model semantics, state the extra causal assumptions, and refuse a stronger interpretation than the supplied information supports. The present decision is to retain the selected release as a reference and a conditional original-worker candidate. It does not enable a native adapter.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources,exec24_bnlearn_contract}

\subsection{Identity, maintenance and a bounded reuse choice}
The selected CRAN mirror revision is 7d44622a52c29c2d666a8661d048e2b5709a7944, dated 17 July 2026, and declares package version 5.2.1. Its DESCRIPTION names Marco Scutari as maintainer, requires R at least 4.4.0 and compiled C17 support, declares GPL version 2 or later, and lists optional packages for graph conversion, inference, precision and regularization. The mirror is a distribution snapshot; its activity is not the author's complete development history or a promise of future support. Installed-library equivalence, transitive terms, build provenance and an operator remain to be qualified.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources}

The Python project with the same name is a distinct repository and distribution. Its pinned README describes Python workflows and links to another inference ecosystem. It is not an alias for the R package or evidence that the same functions, licenses, numerical conventions or objects are interchangeable. A source-identity record must therefore include language, repository, distribution and revision, rather than just the string bnlearn. The current review makes no technical or rights admission for that namesake.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_identity,exec24_bnlearn_contract}

Scutari's original article explains the historical separation between structure learning, local parameter estimation, conditional-independence tests and score-based search. Its discrete and Gaussian examples help motivate an interface that preserves data types and model assumptions. The locally retained author version is arXiv revision 2, dated 10 July 2010, with placeholder journal issue fields; it is not silently relabeled as the publisher's final typesetting. Its 2010 algorithm and computing environment cannot establish the capabilities or behavior of the selected 2026 source.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_original}

\subsection{An equivalent graph is not an equivalent intervention}
The inspected CPDAG documentation distinguishes an equivalence class from one consistent DAG extension. It also explains that parametric restrictions, such as the permitted direction between discrete and continuous variables in a conditional Gaussian model, can preserve directions independently of observational evidence. A returned orientation must therefore carry its provenance: compelled by the observational class, imposed by background knowledge, forced by the model family, or selected as an extension.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources}

An exact authored example shows why this distinction changes a banking decision. Let $A$ be a standardized action intensity and $Y$ a standardized response. Both variables have mean zero, variance one and covariance $1/2$. One Gaussian structural model is
\[
 A=U_A,\qquad Y=\tfrac12 A+U_Y,
 \qquad \operatorname{Var}(U_A)=1,\quad \operatorname{Var}(U_Y)=\tfrac34,
\]
with independent disturbances. Another is
\[
 Y=V_Y,\qquad A=\tfrac12 Y+V_A,
 \qquad \operatorname{Var}(V_Y)=1,\quad \operatorname{Var}(V_A)=\tfrac34,
\]
again with independent disturbances. Both generate exactly the same bivariate Gaussian observation law. Increasing $A$ by one unit changes expected $Y$ by $1/2$ in the first model and by zero in the second. At a stipulated 100 USD per unit response and a 20 USD action cost, the corresponding net values are 30 USD and minus 20 USD. More samples from that unchanged observation law cannot select the correct action. External direction information, a justified restriction or an intervention is needed.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_contract}

The package's causal frontend rejects a partially directed or cyclic graph for the inspected graph-to-SCM, intervention and counterfactual branches. This is a useful syntactic boundary. It does not establish that an accepted DAG is the correct causal graph. The proposed interface retains the CPDAG and the reason for each additional orientation; it never silently turns one consistent extension into an identified effect.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources,exec24_bnlearn_contract}

\subsection{A twin graph needs a specified cross-world model}
The inspected graph conversion creates one exogenous parent per factual node and checks generated labels for collisions. Twin construction shares exogenous nodes between factual and counterfactual copies. For a fitted Gaussian network, the implementation constructs fitted twin components. For other fitted-network classes, the inspected frontend falls back to a graph construction, which does not retain a full fitted counterfactual distribution. A caller must inspect the returned class and content before claiming a numerical counterfactual.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources}

Even an interventional response distribution need not identify an individual's cross-world outcome. Let $A$ and $U$ be independent fair binary variables. In world one set $Y=U$ for either action; in world two set $Y=U\mathbin{\mathrm{xor}}A$. Both worlds give the same observed joint distribution, with probability $1/4$ for each $(A,Y)$ pair, and both give $P(Y=1\mid\operatorname{do}(A=a))=1/2$ for either $a$. For a customer observed with $A=0,Y=0$, however, the counterfactual $Y(1)$ is zero in world one and one in world two. These are explicitly stipulated structural models; the second need not satisfy a faithfulness restriction. Their purpose is to show the information a cross-world claim requires. A convenient twin representation does not supply that information by itself.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_contract}

Intervention values also have a distributional meaning. The selected discrete fitted-node branch gives equal mass to the supplied set of levels. Supplying two levels is therefore not a declaration of an arbitrary 90/10 stochastic intervention. With losses zero and 100 USD, an equal mixture has expected loss 50 USD whereas a 90/10 mixture has expected loss ten. A request for a stochastic policy must carry its probability law and use a qualified path that preserves it.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources,exec24_bnlearn_contract}

A separate static metadata question concerns Gaussian interventions. The inspected branch changes the intercept and sets the standard deviation to zero, but assigns the requested fixed values to the residual field before zeroing that field; it does not assign them to the stored fitted-values field in those lines. With stored fitted values $(1,2)$ and a requested fixed value five, the literal field assignments retain $(1,2)$ while residuals become zero. This is a targeted cache-consistency test for a future native worker, not an observed error in its returned intervention distribution.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources,exec24_bnlearn_contract}

\subsection{Bootstrap strength is a statistic with a sampling law}
The selected bootstrap frontend defaults to 200 replicates, a sample size equal to the number of input rows and conversion to a CPDAG for arc strength. The generic backend samples rows with replacement. A customer's repeated visits or a common calendar shock are therefore not automatically respected as independent sampling units. In an authored mean example with 20 independent customer clusters, 25 visits per customer, unit marginal variance and within-customer correlation 0.6, the mean variance is 0.0308. Treating all 500 visits as independent gives 0.002. That arithmetic is not a coverage theorem for graph search; it explains why a row-resampling option cannot certify the graph's sampling law.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources,exec24_bnlearn_contract}

The inspected C counter gives half weight in each direction to an undirected arc. It computes adjacency strength by adding the two directional counts and direction by dividing one directional count by that adjacency strength. Suppose 100 equally weighted graph replicates contain 20 $A\to Y$ arcs, 40 undirected $A-Y$ arcs and 40 absences. The forward counter is 0.4, the reverse counter 0.2, adjacency strength 0.6 and reported forward direction $2/3$. Only 0.2 of the replicates actually contain the directed arc. None of these quantities is automatically a posterior probability that a causal orientation is true, or a confidence interval for its effect.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources,exec24_bnlearn_contract}

Model averaging adds another transformation: the inspected backend retains strengths strictly above the selected threshold, always retains strength one, filters directions at least 0.5 and invokes a cycle-aware delegate where required. The returned graph and the complete strength table should both survive conversion. Thresholding must not erase unresolved orientations, missing edges or the distinction between stability and causal truth.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources,exec24_bnlearn_contract}

The generic bootstrap's remapping of manipulated observations deserves an independent test. Its inspected branch applies a first-match operation to each original manipulated row index. For an original manipulated row two and a bootstrap index vector $(2,2,3,1)$, that operation returns only position one, whereas the replicate contains manipulated copies at positions one and two. This exact indexing witness motivates a targeted test of the selected score and experiment-label path. It does not claim that every bootstrap algorithm takes that branch or establish an observed end-to-end package failure.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources,exec24_bnlearn_contract}

\subsection{Conditional queries need denominators and retained weights}
The query documentation distinguishes logic sampling from likelihood weighting. Logic sampling retains observations matching the evidence; the number accepted can be much smaller than the number generated. The inspected probability routine returns zero when no simulated observations match. That zero conflates a numerical convention with an unresolved conditional query if an adapter reports it without the accepted-sample denominator. A future worker should return a distinct insufficient-evidence status, its generated and accepted counts and a justified Monte Carlo diagnostic.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources,exec24_bnlearn_contract}

Parallel query aggregation also requires an explicit estimator. The inspected dispatcher takes the mean of worker probability estimates. If one worker has one accepted sample and one event success while another has nine accepted samples and no success, the mean of ratios is $1/2$ but the pooled ratio is $1/10$. These are different finite simulation statistics. This witness does not establish a general bias claim; it requires the product to name the aggregation law rather than suggesting a pooled denominator it did not use.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources,exec24_bnlearn_contract}

Likelihood-weighted samples carry a weights attribute. Dropping that attribute while exporting a table changes the estimate: with outcomes $(0,1)$ and weights $(0.1,0.9)$, the weighted mean is 0.9 and the unweighted mean 0.5. The inspected C distribution sampler explicitly errors on all-zero or all-missing weights, while the R probability path ends with a weighted numerator divided by a weighted denominator. These are distinct failure interfaces to test. The adapter should bind weights, effective information, missingness, random streams and error status, rather than converting every numerical object into a successful causal answer.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources,exec24_bnlearn_contract}

\subsection{A concrete proposal decision and public prototype route}
Retain bnlearn as a reference for graph-class, fitted-model and query contracts. A future pinned R worker is a conditional candidate because it preserves native object semantics more directly than a whole-library port. Defer native admission until exact distribution and dependency rights, graph/class conversions, sampling units, numerical diagnostics and failure behavior are qualified. Reject an automatic DAG-extension-to-causal-effect path, silent weight removal, default zero for an unresolved query and a port justified only by language preference. Alternatives include an independent small exact enumerator, another separately qualified graph learner and a distinct symbolic identification stage.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_contract}

The proposed CLI/MCP request includes graph type and orientation provenance; variable states and units; population and information clock; model family and noise assumptions; fitted parameter identity; observational, interventional or counterfactual target; intervention law; sampling unit; seed, budget and computational limits. The response preserves the source object class, ambiguity, weights, denominators, convergence and refusal status. A typed query language should be translated through an allowlisted expression grammar; source examples that evaluate R expressions do not justify exposing unrestricted expression execution to a general caller.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_sources,exec24_bnlearn_contract}

The next experiments can use generated finite networks and public data with documented permissions. Predeclare graph equivalence, counterfactual-coupling, intervention-mixture, manipulated-row, rare-evidence, parallel aggregation and weighted-export tests. Compare independent exact answers and complete workflows before using authentic bank data. The scientific owner supplies causal and sampling assumptions; the adapter owner supplies conversion and refusal behavior; rights and platform owners bind distributions and dependencies; the evaluation owner records all attempts and complete costs. At a stipulated 1,200 USD total, 100 requests, 80 conformant outputs and 50 useful outputs imply 12, 15 and 24 USD per respective unit. These costs explain denominators; they are not measured prices or adoption evidence.~\citep{exec24_bnlearn_contract}

\section{Tigramite as a temporal reference: preserving the meaning of the result}
\label{exec23:tigramite-scope}
A bank comparing deposit rates and subsequent balances needs to distinguish a conditional-dependence statistic, an unresolved endpoint, a fitted intervention response and uncertainty about that response. Tigramite offers explicitly differentiated methods for these stages. The selected January 2026 source declares version 5.2.10.1. PCMCI, PCMCI+, LPCMCI, RPCMCI and J-PCMCI+ differ in contemporaneous-link, hidden-variable, stationarity and context assumptions. The conditional-independence test adds its own restrictions: ParCorr uses linear residual correlation, whereas other tests require their own treatment. These are distinct scientific choices within one package, not interchangeable routes to a monetary effect. \cite{exec23_tigramite_reuse_source,exec14_pt_tigramite}

The proposal selects Tigramite as a method-specific reference, with a conditional route to an isolated Python worker. Prior installed-version operations remain relevant within their recorded scope, including the finite-DAG oracle comparison. A matching version string does not establish byte identity with the selected repository tree, and those operations do not qualify every delegate considered here. \cite{runtime_tigramite_scope,runtime_networkx_dynamic} The additional evidence below is static source inspection and independent exact examples, not another native trial.

\subsection{A statistic, a graph and a dollar response}
\label{exec23:tigramite-statistic}
Let $X_t$ denote a centered rate intervention in specified rate units, $Y_{t+1}$ a balance response in dollars and $Z_t$ the chosen history. Consider a stipulated linear population,
\begin{equation}
 Y_{t+1}=100+20X_t+\gamma^\top Z_t+\epsilon_{t+1},\qquad
 \operatorname{Var}(X_t\mid Z_t)=1,\quad
 \operatorname{Var}(\epsilon_{t+1}\mid Z_t)=225.
 \label{exec23:tigramite-dollar}
\end{equation}
Assume independent residualized exposure and error, an intervention-invariant mechanism, a history sufficient for adjustment, and intervention support. The residual covariance is $20$ and the residual variance of $Y$ is $625$. Thus the partial correlation is $20/25=4/5$, whereas the stipulated causal slope is 20 dollars per rate unit. A one-unit change with stipulated cost 12 dollars has net value 8 dollars. Reading $4/5$ as a dollar gain instead gives $-56/5$ dollars. These are authored population values, not observed bank returns; their disagreement demonstrates the units lost in an invalid conversion. \cite{exec23_tigramite_reference}

PCMCI labels its \texttt{val\_matrix} as test-statistic values and its optional \texttt{conf\_matrix} as intervals for those values. ParCorr fits ordinary least-squares residuals and returns their Pearson correlation. Its analytic test uses $T-d$ degrees of freedom for $d$ array rows and $T$ usable observations, returning a nonfinite p-value when degrees of freedom are insufficient. The CI base also supplies a constant-variable shortcut. An adapter must report degeneracy and invalid arithmetic rather than interpret every returned entry as scientific evidence of absence. The \texttt{run\_pcmci} signature defaults FDR to \texttt{none}, although the parameter prose says \texttt{fdr\_bh}; a profile must set this argument explicitly. The correction helper distinguishes lagged-only and contemporaneous-inclusive test families. A corrected edge p-value is not a portfolio-response interval. \cite{exec23_tigramite_reuse_source}

\subsection{Endpoint ambiguity is a substantive output}
\label{exec23:tigramite-graph}
PCMCI+ can return contemporaneous ambiguity and conflict marks; LPCMCI returns a temporal partial ancestral graph. The selected effect class accepts DAG and ADMG variants, including temporal and stationary forms, but does not enable its commented-out PAG graph types. A wrapper cannot bridge these interfaces by replacing circles or conflicts with arbitrary arrows. It must retain the compatible graph family, justify additional scientific restrictions where possible, and determine identification or set identification under those restrictions. A graph-conditioned adjustment fit does not solve that preceding problem. \cite{exec23_tigramite_reuse_source}

Even a summary frequency requires its exact definition. The default graph-summary routine chooses a modal endpoint string but, in a tie, reports the sum of tied modal counts divided by the resample count. Five resamples with one direction and five with the reverse can produce a conflict mark with reported frequency one, although none of the ten inputs contains that conflict mark. In a second stipulated ensemble, four absent links and three of each direction make absence the most frequent individual string, while adjacency has total frequency $6/10$. The alternative adjacency-first aggregation answers the latter question. These finite examples do not establish discovery failure; they show why the counts, aggregation rule and emitted mark must accompany the summary. Such frequency is neither the posterior probability of an arrow nor a calibrated graph confidence level. \cite{exec23_tigramite_reuse_source,exec23_tigramite_reference}

\subsection{An intervention prediction still needs a contrast}
\label{exec23:tigramite-intervention}
For a supplied admissible graph and valid adjustment set $Z$, the unconditional adjusted response is
\begin{equation}
 \mu(x)=\int E[Y\mid X=x,Z=z]p(z)\,dz,\qquad
 \Delta(x,x_0)=\mu(x)-\mu(x_0).
 \label{exec23:tigramite-contrast}
\end{equation}
This requires identifying graph and sampling assumptions; fitting regression does not supply them. The inspected prediction delegate aggregates predictions over stored adjustment observations. For an illustrative zero-mean adjusted history in the linear population, predictions at zero and one are 100 and 120 dollars; the contrast is 20 dollars. Calling 120 the gain includes the baseline. A caller must preserve that baseline, adjustment distribution, units, transforms and conditioning target. The effect class checks adjustment validity by default but exposes a bypass for a user-supplied set. An accepted product profile should not silently enable that bypass. \cite{exec23_tigramite_reuse_source,exec23_tigramite_reference}

The hard intervention sets an exposure value; the source's soft option adds an amount to each observed exposure. For nonlinear responses these policies differ. If $Y=X^2$ and the stipulated reference population places equal mass at zero and two, the hard zero-to-one contrast is $1$, whereas adding one to everyone's observed exposure gives
\begin{equation}
 E[(X+1)^2-X^2]=2E[X]+1=3.
 \label{exec23:tigramite-policy}
\end{equation}
Here soft denotes that additive implementation, not every stochastic intervention in causal inference. Conditional nested regression and alternative estimators need separate review. \cite{exec23_tigramite_reuse_source,exec23_tigramite_reference}

The effect bootstrap refits the selected effect procedure and takes percentiles of intervention predictions, retaining the supplied graph. Discovery bootstrap summaries instead concern graphs and statistics. Neither bootstrap code nor a nonempty interval establishes joint validity after graph discovery, graph or policy selection, or under arbitrary dependence. Specify the uncertainty target and sampling law before choosing which stages to resample. \cite{exec23_tigramite_reuse_source}

\subsection{Calendars, masks and resampling belong in the scientific contract}
\label{exec23:tigramite-calendar}
The data layer constructs lagged arrays from integer reference positions, dataset offsets and a cutoff. Its ordinary twice-maximum-lag cutoff and the effect fitter's maximum-lag cutoff can retain different observations. Masks apply to selected variable roles, separately from missing-value handling. Calendar gaps, reporting delays and multiple panels require explicit time-grid conversion and effective-sample records. The earlier inspected RPCMCI implementation replaces a supplied mask, requiring a distinct admission rule. \cite{exec23_tigramite_reuse_source}

Several static edge cases warrant native oracle tests. The selected data-layer bootstrap draws starts from \texttt{arange(n-L)} for $n$ retained positions and block length $L$. For $n=6,L=2$, these are $0,1,2,3$; the complete nonwrapping set includes start 4. Position 5 is absent from the first expression's reachable indices. This enumeration describes the inspected expression, not an executed failure or quantified inference bias. Nonconsecutive retained positions also need not be adjacent calendar times. \cite{exec23_tigramite_reuse_source,exec23_tigramite_reference}

The missing-value extension constructs later position indices without clipping before assignment. Missing position 5 in a six-position array, extended through lag two, yields $5,6,7$; the last two exceed the bounds. The source writes the used-indices attribute after its dataset loop while concatenating samples from all datasets. A multi-panel adapter must verify complete panel provenance rather than assume this attribute records every panel. These observations motivate narrow replay cases and separately reviewed patches; they do not establish that all package analyses fail. \cite{exec23_tigramite_reuse_source,exec23_tigramite_reference}

\subsection{The reuse choice and the work needed to change it}
\label{exec23:tigramite-choice}
Whole-package adoption is premature because method assumptions, graph conversion, uncertainty and these edge cases require distinct contracts. Porting to R, Java or C++ is not justified by a measured benefit here: orientation rules, tests and resampling would acquire a second maintenance burden. A selected Python worker is the candidate to test, preserving endpoints and counts, effective observations and transforms, warnings and failures, and refusing unsupported graph-to-effect requests. These requirements describe proposed capabilities.

The repository declares GPL version 3 or later and contains GPLv3 text. Setup specifies minimum dependencies rather than a locked environment; optional algorithms add packages. This records licensing evidence, not clearance of a future distribution. Process isolation alone does not settle rights. A maintainer must bind artifacts and versions; a scientific owner must choose the sampling law, target and tests; an operator must address custody, failure budgets and distribution. Comparison should include installation, search, ambiguous and failed queries, resampling, serialization, upgrade regression and human review. Reconsider integration when a permissible pinned profile passes the exact oracles and registered public or synthetic trial. Later bank experiments should be justified by prototype merit rather than required to finish this proposal decision. \cite{exec23_tigramite_reuse_source,exec23_tigramite_reference}

\section{Identification software must preserve what is and is not identified}
\label{exec18:r-identification}

Suppose a bank is considering an encouragement to make an additional repayment. Let $A$ denote randomized access to a reminder and $Y$ denote repayment by twelve months. The business question may ask for the effect of offering the reminder, whether a particular customer would repay only if reminded, or the range of possible repayment effects when an assumption is unavailable. Those are three different targets. A fitted conditional association, an interventional functional, a cross-world probability and a sharp identified set do not have interchangeable meanings. The proposed interface must name the target and information regime before it calls an R package.

For a supplied directed acyclic graph, \texttt{dagitty} can enumerate sufficient adjustment sets under its documented graphical criteria. That is a useful graph diagnostic; it is not a general algorithm for deriving every interventional functional under latent confounding. The existing \texttt{pcalg} and \texttt{bnlearn} comparison adds equivalence-class and causal-network procedures, but their returned graph or effect list has its own model class and assumptions. In particular, \texttt{pcalg}'s IDA manual assumes a faithful multivariate Gaussian law and a causal DAG without hidden variables, and estimates total effects over admissible DAG extensions. It is neither an arbitrary nonlinear effect region nor a confidence set for a bank decision. The \texttt{ivmodel} confidence-set interface answers another question again: its documented AR/CLR inversion returns interval components under its declared IV design, with CLR described as robust to weak instruments. The proposed wrapper must preserve these set components and admit infinite endpoints where the inversion yields them. A single label such as “causal effect” would erase these distinctions~\citep{exec13_other_dagitty,exec13_pcalg,exec13_bnlearn,v12_sw_ivmodel}.

The R package \texttt{causaleffect} supplies symbolic ID/IDC functionality and selected transportability and selection-recovery procedures. Its pinned R function accepts outcome, treatment, optional conditioning variables and a graph; it can return an expression or a derivation object. The selected implementation has an important adapter consequence: with its default \texttt{stop\_on\_nonid=TRUE}, nonidentification raises an error; with the option false, a failed identification can be represented by \texttt{id=FALSE} in the step result, an empty string, or \texttt{NULL}, depending on the other output options. A production wrapper must distinguish that documented algorithmic result from parse errors, unsupported graphs and runtime exceptions. It should also retain the graph class and functional, not just a Boolean or formatted string~\citep{exec18_rid_causaleffect}.

The pinned \texttt{dosearch} package asks whether an interventional target follows from multiple observational or experimental distributions and a graph, including selected transport, selection and missingness mechanisms. Its 2021 paper describes a sound search over do-calculus rules, with completeness tied to the specified problem class. Its Table 1 distinguishes complete ID/IDC cases, unknown completeness for general multiple-source identification, and an incomplete missing-data extension. The package documentation expressly says its missing-data search is not complete. More subtly, the pinned C++ source applies a positive time limit by returning early from the search; its initializer then decides identifiability from whether a target distribution was found and does not expose a separate timeout state in that result. Therefore, under that code path, a limit-truncated search can be represented like “not identifiable by this search.” An adapter must not promote that output to a proof of nonidentifiability. It must either disable the limit for the bounded admitted problem, instrument a distinct timeout/unknown state, or report the uncompleted search as unknown. This is a source-level review of a pinned revision, not an executed package trial~\citep{exec18_rid_dosearch,exec18_rid_dosearch_paper}.

Counterfactual identification is a further level. \texttt{cfid} implements ID*/IDC* for counterfactual conjunctions by constructing a parallel-worlds graph with shared latent variables and then applying recursive identification. Its function defaults to identifying from interventions, not from observations; the observational level must be requested or obtained through the documented alternatives. A true \texttt{id} field is not sufficient on its own: an inconsistent conjunction may be identifiable with probability zero, and a conditional query may be undefined when its conditioning event has zero probability. An adapter therefore retains the requested data level, identifying formula, returned \texttt{undefined} field and query itself; an inconsistency represented by a zero formula must retain that meaning. The package paper explains why shared latent variables connect the counterfactual worlds; treating them as independent simulated populations would change the query~\citep{exec18_rid_cfid,exec18_rid_cfid_paper}.

When point identification fails, bounds may still be useful, but their scope is not universal. \texttt{causaloptim} encodes certain graph-defined response-function problems as linear programs and permits added constraints; its documented graph attributes and admissible left/right structure delimit the class. \texttt{bpbounds} implements binary treatment and outcome bounds for an instrument with two or three categories, in a single trivariate table or a specified two-source bivariate design, with separate monotonicity outputs. Neither manual claims to deliver a sampling confidence region for every reported plug-in endpoint. The distinction matters: an identified set describes values compatible with a population law and assumptions; sampling uncertainty concerns how an estimator or set varies across repeated samples. A posterior interval from a separate Bayesian model is different again. The software contract should return these objects with different types and labels, including empty, unbounded, undefined and unknown states where relevant~\citep{exec18_rid_causaloptim,exec18_rid_bpbounds}.

\texttt{CausalQueries} is a useful adjacent tool rather than a substitute for a graphical identification test. Its documentation defines binary causal models, updates model parameters and supports queries over priors, parameters or posterior draws. Its query manual calls the default interval a “confidence interval” under the statistics argument, but names the interval endpoints as credible summaries and allows a prior or posterior distribution as the input. The proposed interface must attach the distribution and uncertainty meaning explicitly instead of inheriting that ambiguous label. Such a posterior answers a question conditional on the declared graph, response-type parameterization, restrictions, prior and update model. It need not equal a likelihood-identified set, and a narrow credible interval cannot certify that omitted graphs or cross-world dependence have been resolved. For the proposed library, the package is a reference for probabilistic model-query syntax and an optional R adapter candidate. The described package and its compiled Stan dependencies have not been installed, executed or cleared for redistribution in this review~\citep{exec18_rid_causalqueries}.

\subsection{An exact repayment example separates four outputs}

Let $Y_0,Y_1\in\{0,1\}$ be repayment under no reminder and reminder. Randomization identifies $\Pr(Y_0=1)=1/4$ and $\Pr(Y_1=1)=3/4$ in this deliberately stipulated population example. Write $q_{ij}=\Pr(Y_0=i,Y_1=j)$ and let $t=q_{11}$. The observed arm risks imply
\begin{align}
 q_{11}&=t, &q_{10}&=\frac14-t,\notag\\
 q_{01}&=\frac34-t, &q_{00}&=t,\qquad 0\leq t\leq\frac14. \label{exec18:r-fiber}
\end{align}
The intervention effect is point identified throughout this fiber:
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{ATE}=\mathbb E(Y_1-Y_0)=\frac34-\frac14=\frac12.
 \label{exec18:r-ate}
\end{equation}
But the retrospective “necessity” fraction among would-be responders under the reminder is
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{PN}=\Pr(Y_0=0\mid Y_1=1)
 =\frac{q_{01}}{q_{01}+q_{11}}=1-\frac{4t}{3}\in\left[\frac23,1\right].
 \label{exec18:r-pn}
\end{equation}
The average effect is a point, while the counterfactual fraction is only set identified. Assuming monotonicity $Y_1\geq Y_0$ removes the response type $q_{10}$, forces $t=1/4$ and narrows PN to $2/3$. That is an added scientific restriction, not information generated by a software call.

Every value in this interval is attainable: choose the corresponding $t$ and sample the four response types with probabilities $q_{ij}$, independently of randomized assignment. This supplies a constructive sharpness argument. The population risks above are stipulated constants; observing one success versus three in two samples of four would not establish those risks without sampling uncertainty.

The same distinction survives Bayesian updating. Consider the three compatible worlds $t\in\{0,1/8,1/4\}$. With four randomized units in each arm and the stated one-of-four versus three-of-four successes, the likelihood of the ordered outcomes is $(1/4)^2(3/4)^6=729/65536$ in every world. Thus the data do not change their relative weights. A uniform prior on these three worlds gives posterior mean PN $5/6$; prior weights $(1/6,1/3,1/2)$ give $7/9$. These are exact project calculations, not output from \texttt{CausalQueries} and not a defect in Bayesian computation: the difference records prior sensitivity along a likelihood-flat fiber. The aggregate binomial likelihood is $729/4096$ in every world, including the common combinatorial factor.

For an illustrative retrospective accounting rule, assign USD 1,000 to a responder whose repayment is attributed to the reminder and subtract USD 800 per responder. The compatible net amount per responder ranges from $1000(2/3)-800=-\$400/3$ to $1000(1)-800=\$200$. The two posterior means are USD $1000(5/6)-800=100/3$ and USD $1000(7/9)-800=-200/9$, respectively. These stipulated amounts are not observed economics or ROI, and attribution after the outcome must not be misread as prospective treatment selection on future repayment. The executable Fraction reference enumerates all three response-type distributions and checks every displayed likelihood and monetary value~\citep{exec18_rid_exact}.

\subsection{Conditional reuse and the boundary of a negative answer}

The lending case makes the graph restriction concrete. Suppose unrecorded risk $U$ affects both the offer $A$ and repayment $Y$. In a deliberately simple example, take $U\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(1/2)$ and $A=U$. Two structural models, $Y=A$ and $Y=U$, give the same observed $A=Y$ distribution, yet their average effects of intervening on $A$ are respectively one and zero. The graph allowing $U\to A$, $U\to Y$ and $A\to Y$ contains both models, because an allowed parent may have zero effect. No precision of fitting that observed distribution selects the intervention answer. This is an identification failure relative to that information and graph class. The earlier randomized example changes the information and identifies the average effect, while leaving cross-world attribution ambiguous.

For this use, the proposed adapter result retains three epistemic states: identified with a functional or point; not identified by a complete algorithm for its declared graph/data class; and unknown because the class is unsupported or computation ended before a certificate. Invalid input is a separate validation failure, with no scientific conclusion. Sharp identified sets, sampling confidence sets and Bayesian posterior summaries have separately named result types and assumptions. A zero-probability conditioning event is an undefined query, not a negative identification certificate.

The practical proposal is to retain \texttt{causaleffect} as a candidate symbolic R service and \texttt{cfid} as a counterfactual-query service, preserving their query objects and derivations. \texttt{dosearch} complements those services for multiple source distributions, but an adapter must record admitted search rules and termination before interpreting a negative result. Disabling a time limit does not make an incomplete rule system complete. Porting any of these algorithms requires an independently checked mathematical contract and differential fixtures; matching a formatted expression alone would miss a changed conditioning event or data level. For that reason direct source ports are deferred while optional process-bound R adapters are designed. This is a proposed interface boundary, not an executed integration or a conclusion about license compatibility~\citep{exec18_rid_causaleffect,exec18_rid_cfid,exec18_rid_dosearch}.

The restricted bounds engines offer a different reuse choice. \texttt{causaloptim} is a candidate optimization reference when its response-function class is appropriate; \texttt{bpbounds} supplies a smaller binary-IV reference with explicit monotonicity and two-source assumptions. The proposal rejects either as a universal sharp-bounds backend. It retains \texttt{CausalQueries} as an optional model-query path when priors and sensitivity are substantively justified, while rejecting a posterior interval as a replacement for identification. Dependency and license observations inform these choices but do not qualify redistribution, linked dependencies, numerical behavior or installed package execution~\citep{exec18_rid_causaloptim,exec18_rid_bpbounds,exec18_rid_causalqueries}.

These comparisons establish distinct candidate roles and failure semantics. Coverage of the wider R ecosystem remains incomplete; selected packages and their inspected versions do not exhaust the available methods. The exact examples establish the stated mathematical distinctions and illustrative accounting consequences, while implementation and broader source coverage remain future work.

\part{Estimating effects and comparing existing tools}
\label{reading:part-effects}
An identified effect still needs an estimator, a suitable comparison group
and a defensible account of uncertainty. The choice differs for an invitation,
an eligibility threshold, a repeated servicing rule and a branch rollout.
Commercial and open-source alternatives are assessed against those distinctions.

\section{Native causal forests: preserve the target before accelerating it}
\label{exec21:cxx-scope}
A servicing team considering different offers for different risk groups needs more than an accurate forecast of repayment. It needs a contrast between actions, a declared population over which to average that contrast, and uncertainty that corresponds to the decision. A C++ implementation can reduce computation while changing any of these meanings at a language boundary. The useful comparison therefore starts with the statistical object and follows it through wrappers, native learning, stored draws and portfolio aggregation. Four inspected lineages provide complementary designs: generalized random forests, \texttt{bcf}, XBCF and flexBCF. Their common use of trees does not make their targets, priors or intervals interchangeable.~\citep{exec21_cxx_grf,exec21_cxx_bcf,exec21_cxx_xbcf,exec21_cxx_flexbcf}

The comparison binds grf 2.6.1 to its 30 April 2026 commit, bcf 2.0.2 to 26 September 2023, XBCF's declared 0.1.2 source to 25 June 2024, and flexBCF 1.0.3 to 1 December 2025. These are inspected versions preceding the literature cutoff, not assertions about the newest release. The native grf boundary analysis developed elsewhere in this monograph remains applicable: the initial proposal favors a qualified R process around its native core, rather than a new direct embedding or port. This comparison extends the alternatives and semantic contracts; it supplies no runtime qualification.~\citep{exec21_cxx_grf,exec21_cxx_bcf,exec21_cxx_xbcf,exec21_cxx_flexbcf}

\subsection{A conditional effect and a dose slope answer different questions}
\label{exec21:cxx-target}
For a binary action $A$, let $Y(a)$ be the potential outcome under action $a$ and $X$ permitted baseline information. Under consistency, no interference, conditional ignorability and overlap, the conditional average effect is
\begin{equation}
 \tau(x)=E\{Y(1)-Y(0)\mid X=x\}.
 \label{exec21:cxx-cate}
\end{equation}
The inspected grf wrapper describes this binary target, estimates or accepts aligned outcome and treatment nuisance predictions, and residualizes before forest fitting. Honesty separates split selection from leaf estimation. The native trainer checks whether the sample and honesty fractions leave observations in both parts; its tree trainer splits sampled clusters and repopulates estimation leaves. Computational separation does not establish ignorability or validate a treatment clock.~\citep{exec21_cxx_grf}

For continuous treatment the wrapper documents a conditional covariance-to-variance slope,
\begin{equation}
 s(x)=\frac{\operatorname{Cov}(Y,A\mid X=x)}{\operatorname{Var}(A\mid X=x)}.
 \label{exec21:cxx-slope}
\end{equation}
It cannot be substituted automatically for $E\{Y(a_1)-Y(a_0)\mid X=x\}$. In an authored example, randomize $A$ uniformly over $\{-1,0,1\}$ and set $Y(a)=a^2$. The covariance numerator is zero by symmetry, while the action contrast from zero to one is one. Neither confounding nor a computational failure is needed for the discrepancy: the requested objects differ. A bank seeking the response to a specific change in fee or service intensity must request that dose pair or justify a response model under which the slope represents it. This is an exact semantic witness, not a run of grf.~\citep{exec21_cxx_grf,exec21_cxx_reference}

The native prediction strategy solves a weighted local moment ratio. Its instrumental implementation divides an outcome--instrument covariance expression by a treatment--instrument expression. A near-zero denominator is a scientific and numerical warning. The inspected variance code groups trees, skips groups with empty leaves and corrects between- and within-group quantities. Its objective-Bayes correction avoids negative variance estimates; that label does not make its output draws from a Bayesian causal forest. An adapter must retain effective group counts, denominator diagnostics and unsupported variance status. Full upstream guard behavior requires a qualified native trial.~\citep{exec21_cxx_grf}

\subsection{Why regularizing prediction can distort a treatment contrast}
\label{exec21:cxx-prior}
The genuine inspected BCF original is the 33-page arXiv version 4 of 13 November 2019 by Hahn, Murray and Carvalho; it is not asserted to be byte-identical to the later journal article. Under its stated assumptions the conditional mean is modeled as
\begin{equation}
 E(Y\mid X=x,A=a)=\mu\{x,\widehat\pi(x)\}+a\tau(x),
 \label{exec21:cxx-bcf-model}
\end{equation}
with separate tree priors for the prognostic and treatment functions. The fitted propensity $\widehat\pi$ informs the response-surface prior. This addresses regularization-induced confounding: shrinkage of the prognostic function can leave treatment to explain outcome variation associated with selection. A propensity feature does not imply an orthogonal estimating equation or double robustness. The paper explicitly disclaims double robustness for its natural Bayesian estimates and discusses sensitivity to alternative propensity estimates.~\citep{exec21_cxx_hahn}

A project derivation isolates the mechanism. Let $X$ be equally likely to be $-1$ or $1$, with treatment probabilities $1/4$ and $3/4$ respectively. Set $Y(a)=2X$ for both actions, so the true causal effect is zero and overlap holds. Write $Z=A-1/2$. Minimize
\begin{equation}
 E\{(Y-\beta X-tZ)^2\}+\lambda\beta^2,
 \qquad \lambda=3/4.
 \label{exec21:cxx-shrink-objective}
\end{equation}
Only the prognostic coefficient is penalized. Here $E(X^2)=1$, $E(Z^2)=1/4$ and $E(XZ)=1/4$. The treatment normal equation gives $t=2-\beta$; the prognostic equation then gives
\begin{equation}
 \beta=\frac{2(3/4)}{3/4+\lambda}=1,
 \qquad t=1.
 \label{exec21:cxx-shrink-result}
\end{equation}
The apparent treatment effect is produced by the stipulated shrinkage. The exact reference verifies both normal equations. This motivates studying prior design, treatment selection and nuisance learning jointly. It is not a theorem that every regularized learner fails or that BCF necessarily repairs every selection mechanism.~\citep{exec21_cxx_reference,exec21_cxx_hahn}

The bcf wrapper exposes separate prognostic and moderation matrices, supplied propensities and their placement in either or both ensembles. Prior-scale choices and outcome standardization belong to the fitted model. A port that copies trees but drops a scale prior changes the procedure. flexBCF explicitly distinguishes its default model from bcf in scale priors, treatment-recoding behavior and relative regularization of the two ensembles. A claim of speed or flexibility therefore cannot establish identical inference. A prototype comparison must freeze each configuration and compare a common target.~\citep{exec21_cxx_bcf,exec21_cxx_flexbcf}

\subsection{Three meanings of weight must remain separate}
\label{exec21:cxx-weights}
The bcf argument \texttt{w} represents likelihood precision: the conditional variance is $\sigma^2/w_i$. The native source uses these weights in tree updates and residual sums. They do not define the population for a reported effect. For a declared cohort $\mathcal I$ and target weights $q_i$, a draw-wise average is
\begin{equation}
 T^{(s)}=\frac{\sum_{i\in\mathcal I}q_i\tau_i^{(s)}}{\sum_{i\in\mathcal I}q_i}.
 \label{exec21:cxx-aggregate}
\end{equation}
Likelihood precision, target weights and cohort selection require different fields. The target can concern eligible customers, treated customers or a transported population; its meaning cannot be recovered from an unlabeled vector.~\citep{exec21_cxx_bcf,exec21_cxx_flexbcf}

For two equally represented groups with effects $-2$ and $6$, the population effect is $2$. Reusing precisions $1$ and $9$ as target weights gives $26/5=5.2$. If these effects are stipulated dollar gains and the offer costs USD 3 per customer, the population margin is USD -1 while the reweighted margin is USD 2.20. Better observation precision does not authorize replacing the eligible customer mix.~\citep{exec21_cxx_reference}

In grf, equalizing cluster weights changes training samples and average-effect weights. The documented training rule draws equal numbers of units from sampled clusters, using the smallest cluster size. This option is incompatible with supplying the ordinary sample-weight vector in that interface. Balancing branches instead of customers changes both the target and parts of learning; it cannot be implemented by relabeling a final average while leaving an incompatible training run untouched.~\citep{exec21_cxx_grf}

\subsection{Joint draws, scale restoration and persistence}
\label{exec21:cxx-draws}
A Bayesian portfolio interval averages customer effects within a common posterior draw before summarizing portfolio draws. Averaging individual interval endpoints generally differs. Consider two equally weighted customers with joint draws $(-2,6)$ and $(6,-2)$. Each customer's marginal support is $[-2,6]$, but every portfolio draw is exactly $2$. Averaging marginal endpoints discards the perfect negative dependence. At the assumed USD 3 cost every portfolio margin is USD -1. Two artificial draws illustrate algebra; they establish neither convergence nor calibration.~\citep{exec21_cxx_reference}

The inspected flexBCF native SATT routine evaluates each stored ensemble on supplied rows, averages within the draw and then calculates quantiles. Its weighted companion divides by the supplied weight sum. The routine name does not select treated rows: cohort selection must already be reflected in its inputs. These selected functions do not settle how all public callers validate empty cohorts, nonfinite weights, zero sums or category levels. The proposed adapter must require explicit cohort and weight contracts; complete caller tracing remains open.~\citep{exec21_cxx_flexbcf}

Scale parameters must remain aligned by draw. In inspected bcf prediction an ensemble output $g_i^{(s)}$ contributes
\begin{equation}
 \tau_i^{(s)}=s_Y\{b_1^{(s)}-b_0^{(s)}\}g_i^{(s)},
 \label{exec21:cxx-restore}
\end{equation}
where $s_Y$ is the outcome scale. A treatment contrast receives no added outcome mean. XBCF also restores a treatment draw using outcome scale and its treatment-scale difference. If raw draws are $1,2$, $s_Y=10$, and scale differences are $1/2,2$, restored draws are $5,40$ with mean $22.5$. Omitting the contrast factor gives mean $15$. At an assumed USD 20 action cost these imply USD 2.50 and USD -5 margins. Multiplying mean raw output by mean scale also loses their posterior dependence.~\citep{exec21_cxx_bcf,exec21_cxx_xbcf,exec21_cxx_reference}

bcf documents draws by units, whereas selected XBCF outputs use units by iterations. Transport needs unit, chain and retained-draw identifiers rather than a guessed transpose. bcf's wrapper serializes tree samples for later prediction; suppressing file output prevents that path. Its selected prediction wrapper requires both treatment labels, a restriction to reconcile for a one-cohort request. The chain executor sets one core while retaining within-chain thread controls. These are persistence and execution questions for an adapter, not measured speed or scalability.~\citep{exec21_cxx_bcf,exec21_cxx_xbcf}

\subsection{Static hazards define focused prototype tests}
\label{exec21:cxx-hazards}
In pinned XBCF's combined R predictor, the default propensity branch refers to \texttt{z}, which is not a formal argument. The function assigns predictions to \texttt{obj1} and \texttt{obj2}, then reads \texttt{obj} before its local assignment at the end. The separate treatment-draw predictor uses its own local object. These are particular source paths requiring isolated reconciliation, not failures observed by executing the package. An enclosing environment, later release or different entrypoint must not silently replace the inspected route.~\citep{exec21_cxx_xbcf}

In the selected Python wrapper, the zero-outcome-variance branch constructs \texttt{ValueError} without raising it, and adjusted fitted draws are assigned inside the standardization branch. Prediction has separate restoration logic. Tests should cover constant outcomes, both standardization settings, fit-versus-predict parity and return formats. The native loop alternates prognostic and treatment updates and updates scales; stored output must be checked against wrapper order. Source resemblance does not prove equivalent R and Python interfaces.~\citep{exec21_cxx_xbcf}

The selected flexBCF trainer derives the control count from prognostic and treatment design sizes and offsets treatment updates accordingly. Its wrapper transposes inputs and passes the treatment indicator. Training order and treatment-specific rows therefore require tracing through public callers and documentation. Assuming the same row layout across all four libraries would hide a consequential boundary error.~\citep{exec21_cxx_flexbcf}

\subsection{Historical performance supports an experiment}
\label{exec21:cxx-evidence}
The BCF paper offers evidence for its design and relevant limitations. Its targeted-selection example improves bias and coverage over ordinary BART under that simulation; broader experiments do not give uniformly nominal coverage. In the retrospective ACIC 2016 comparison, Table 4 reports nominal 95\% interval coverage of 82\% for BCF, 88\% for propensity-score BART, 81\% for BART and 58\% for the compared causal random forest. BCF's mean interval length is 0.026 versus 0.055 for that forest. Shorter intervals and lower estimation error coexist with undercoverage. The paper discusses this limitation and supplies no general frequentist coverage theorem for its credible intervals.~\citep{exec21_cxx_hahn}

These are historical reported results, not a project reproduction or ranking of today's implementations. They motivate public experiments reporting effect error, coverage, width, failures and decision consequences together. Known counterfactual generators can vary overlap, targeted selection, effect sparsity, response nonlinearity, propensity misspecification and dependence. Public benchmarks provide complementary designs whose versions, targets and access conditions must be fixed. Failed fits and invalid requests stay in the denominator. Posterior uncertainty, Monte Carlo error and repeated-sampling coverage must remain separate: more draws do not repair a wrong causal target or unmeasured confounding.~\citep{exec21_cxx_hahn,exec21_cxx_reference}

\subsection{Integration choices and the remaining census}
\label{exec21:cxx-integration}
For the proposal, grf remains a candidate through its analyzed R/native boundary. bcf is a candidate for a process-based Bayesian adapter with explicit priors and persisted trees. XBCF warrants a performance comparison after selected wrapper paths are reconciled. flexBCF is a distinct alternative needing prior, coding, training-order and cohort contracts. Porting would transfer responsibility for random numbers, tree updates, priors, draw alignment and persistence. The initial approach should preserve each native model behind a typed adapter and qualify a narrow target before considering a port. These are reasoned proposal choices, not completed reuse admissions.~\citep{exec21_cxx_grf,exec21_cxx_bcf,exec21_cxx_xbcf,exec21_cxx_flexbcf}

Inspected declarations are GPL-family terms for grf, bcf and flexBCF and Apache 2.0 for XBCF. They identify a rights review rather than completing one: dependencies, notices, distribution and embedding remain to be examined. A separate process does not automatically settle licensing obligations. Bounded repository discovery also found SparseBCF, time-series, multivariate, survival and other candidates with CRAN mirrors. Names are leads; lineage, cutoff availability, originals and capabilities remain uninspected. Mirrors do not count as independent implementations.~\citep{exec21_cxx_grf,exec21_cxx_bcf,exec21_cxx_xbcf,exec21_cxx_flexbcf}

Library, CLI and MCP requests should carry action contrast or slope, cohort, clock, source version, nuisance provenance, weight roles, uncertainty type and refusal reasons. An agent can help formulate requests and inspect diagnostics, but cannot relabel posterior quantiles as calibrated confidence limits. These exact witnesses justify inexpensive public prototype tests before costly bank trials. Broader coverage, method originals, rights, native parity and calibrated decision performance remain prerequisites for a stronger adoption claim.

\section{Reusing the R forest package: nuisance provenance and population choice}
\label{exec21:grfr-scope}
A bank considering a retention offer needs an effect for the customers it intends to contact. The R package grf is a useful reference candidate because its wrapper connects nuisance fitting, forest prediction and average-effect estimation. That wrapper is part of the statistical method. A proposed CLI or MCP interface must preserve its population and score construction when exposing the underlying C++ engine. The R package and native core share one implementation lineage; their different deployment routes do not supply independent corroboration. We select the original R package as a bounded reference candidate, with an isolated worker considered for a future prototype. The earlier native-boundary analysis explains the ownership and persistence obligations; here the additional concern is the meaning of supplied nuisance predictions and target options.~\citep{exec21_grf_reuse,exec13_grf_memory}

The selected source declares version 2.6.1 at the 30 April 2026 commit. Its requirements include R 3.5.0 or later, C++17, GNU make, Rcpp and RcppEigen, with imports including DiceKriging, lmtest, Matrix and sandwich. A named maintainer and issue tracker establish a maintenance contact route, not a service commitment. The DESCRIPTION declares GPL-3; file-level terms and dependencies need to accompany any distribution decision. The inspected license distinguishes running a program from conveying it. An isolated process is an engineering boundary and does not by itself settle the licensing of a distributed system. These observations justify retaining the original as a reference without claiming a qualified installed environment.~\citep{exec21_grf_reuse}

\subsection{What the nuisance inputs must mean}
\label{exec21:grfr-nuisance}
Let $A\in\{0,1\}$ denote the offer, $X$ information available before assignment, and $Y$ the outcome over a fixed horizon. Define $e(x)=P(A=1\mid X=x)$ and $\mu_a(x)=E[Y\mid A=a,X=x]$. Consistency, no interference, conditional exchangeability and positivity give $\tau(x)=\mu_1(x)-\mu_0(x)$ the causal interpretation. These are substantive assumptions about assignment and observation; fitting the forest does not verify them.

The wrapper's outcome nuisance is the marginal regression $m(x)=E[Y\mid X=x]=(1-e(x))\mu_0(x)+e(x)\mu_1(x)$. Missing nuisance vectors are estimated by auxiliary regression forests. Supplied scalar values are replicated, and supplied vectors are checked for length. Such checks cannot establish that a prediction uses only permitted information or that it was estimated without access to the evaluation outcome. The interface therefore needs the feature clock, row identity and fitting provenance as well as the numbers. Initially, the proposed worker retains the package's ordinary nuisance path or an explicitly known randomized propensity; externally fitted nuisance vectors require a separate qualification profile.~\citep{exec21_grf_reuse}

Write $m^\star,e^\star,t^\star$ for fixed proposed nuisance functions, with $0<e^\star<1$. The binary score reconstructed by the source is
\begin{equation}
 \Gamma=t^\star+\frac{A-e^\star}{e^\star(1-e^\star)}
       \{Y-m^\star-t^\star(A-e^\star)\}.
 \label{exec21:grfr-score}
\end{equation}
Suppressing $x$, define $u_0^\star=m^\star-e^\star t^\star$ and
$u_1^\star=m^\star+(1-e^\star)t^\star$. Substituting these definitions yields
$\Gamma=u_1^\star-u_0^\star+A(Y-u_1^\star)/e^\star
-(1-A)(Y-u_0^\star)/(1-e^\star)$. For a fresh observation, conditional on these fixed functions, direct expectation gives the project identity
\begin{equation}
 E[\Gamma\mid X]-\tau
 =(e^\star-e)\left\{\frac{u_1^\star-\mu_1}{e^\star}
                   +\frac{u_0^\star-\mu_0}{1-e^\star}\right\}.
 \label{exec21:grfr-bias}
\end{equation}
Indeed, the two residual terms have conditional expectations
$e(\mu_1-u_1^\star)/e^\star$ and
$(1-e)(\mu_0-u_0^\star)/(1-e^\star)$, and collecting errors gives the identity. The conditional bias vanishes when the propensity is correct or when both reconstructed treatment-specific regressions are correct. Correctness of $m^\star$ alone is a different condition. Estimation rates, sample reuse and dependence still govern whether a fitted procedure has valid uncertainty; this fixed-function calculation supplies no finite-sample guarantee.~\citep{exec21_grf_reuse,exec21_grf_reference}

Consider a deliberately simple law with no varying $X$: $P(A=1)=1/4$, $Y(0)=0$ and $Y(1)=2$. The true marginal outcome mean is $1/2$ and the effect is two. Supply the correct values $m^\star=1/2$ and $t^\star=2$, but use $e^\star=1/2$. The reconstructed regressions become $-1/2$ and $3/2$, although the true regressions are zero and two. Equation~\eqref{exec21:grfr-score} gives scores one for controls and three for treated observations, so their population mean is $3/2$. Even a correct marginal outcome regression and correct conditional-effect input do not restore the intended mean in this example.

For comparison, the correct propensity $e^\star=1/4$ with deliberately wrong $m^\star=3$ and $t^\star=0$ produces scores four and minus four, whose mean is two. Alternatively, keeping the wrong propensity $1/2$ but setting $m^\star=1$ and $t^\star=2$ reconstructs the correct outcome surfaces and makes both scores two. Exact rational enumeration checks these cases and the bias identity on 108 combinations. These are supplied-input witnesses, not claims that the fitted grf algorithm generates these nuisance values. Their practical purpose is to prevent an adapter from equating a correct outcome model with an unlabeled marginal prediction vector.~\citep{exec21_grf_reference}

\subsection{An overlap recommendation changes the target}
\label{exec21:grfr-target}
The function \texttt{average\_treatment\_effect} exposes all, treated, control and overlap populations. Under the preceding causal assumptions, their common representation is
\begin{equation}
 \theta_q=\frac{E[q(X)\tau(X)]}{E[q(X)]},\qquad
 q(X)\in\{1,e(X),1-e(X),e(X)(1-e(X))\}.
 \label{exec21:grfr-populations}
\end{equation}
All denominators must be positive. The source warns when estimated propensities become extreme and suggests alternative targets or filtering. A warning does not prove a failure of identification, establish a universal threshold or authorize changing the requested population. A business owner deciding about all eligible customers must explicitly reconsider the question before receiving an effect for customers with greater assignment overlap.~\citep{exec21_grf_reuse}

In a two-group hypothetical bank, let each group contain half the eligible population. Treatment propensities are $1/2$ and $1/10$, and group effects are USD $-4$ and USD 12. The all-customer effect is USD 4. The treated and control population effects are respectively USD $-4/3$ and USD $44/7$. Overlap weights are $1/4$ and $9/100$, giving
\begin{equation}
 \theta_{\rm overlap}
 =\frac{(1/4)(-4)+(9/100)12}{1/4+9/100}
 =\frac{4}{17}.
 \label{exec21:grfr-overlap}
\end{equation}
With an assumed offer cost of USD 2 per recipient, the all-customer margin is USD 2, while the overlap-population margin is USD $-30/17$, approximately USD $-1.76$. Both calculations are correct for their stated populations. Applying the second margin to the original customer population would be a change of decision target, not merely a more stable computation of the same quantity. These dollar figures are reproducible assumptions and do not measure a bank's return.~\citep{exec21_grf_reference}

The implementation also makes the method label consequential. For the all-population AIPW path, it aggregates scores and computes centered cluster sums for the standard error. The overlap path instead regresses the outcome residual on the treatment residual, with an intercept and observation weights, and uses a sandwich variance. Inside that branch the AIPW/TMLE method choice is ignored, although the earlier TMLE guard rejects clustered or sample-weighted inputs. A wrapper should report the actual residual-regression method and preserve the combination restrictions. It should not label an overlap result as an all-population augmented estimate merely because the request said AIPW.~\citep{exec21_grf_reuse}

The population logic of that residual regression can be checked without executing the package. With correct $m$ and $e$, the treatment residual has conditional mean zero, and
$E[(A-e)(Y-m)\mid X]=e(1-e)\tau(X)$ while
$E[(A-e)^2\mid X]=e(1-e)$. The population regression coefficient therefore equals the overlap effect in Equation~\eqref{exec21:grfr-populations}, provided its denominator is nonzero. This argument explains the target. It does not prove that estimated residuals, a particular sandwich correction or a small sample deliver calibrated intervals.

\subsection{Qualification follows the complete statistical request}
\label{exec21:grfr-contract}
The initial proposal profile is one binary action and complete independent customer rows, with a fixed outcome horizon and all-population AIPW target. Clusters, sample weights, continuous actions, instrumental variables, survival, multiarm comparisons and repeated policies need separate profiles. The source exposes several of these methods, but their presence in a dispatcher does not transfer their assumptions or uncertainty guarantees to the initial bank use. A cohort chosen after observing treatment or outcome also changes the evaluation problem; the documented subset warning recommends selection using baseline features. Adaptive selection using fitted effects needs its own honest evaluation design.~\citep{exec21_grf_reuse}

Prediction outputs need equally careful typing. With no \texttt{newdata}, the causal-forest wrapper uses stored or recomputed out-of-bag predictions; explicitly supplying the training matrix as new data follows a different prediction path. Those calls are not interchangeable evidence of sample separation. The documented CATE variance, R-loss diagnostic and Monte Carlo excess error answer different questions. Increasing tree count can address forest simulation instability without creating exchangeability, recovering unsupported customers or resolving dependence. Model serialization must also account for the stored original observations and nuisance vectors.~\citep{exec21_grf_reuse}

The future worker should retain target identity, actual estimator, customer keys, column order, units, time horizon, warnings, source/dependency versions and nuisance provenance. It must distinguish unavailable execution, an unsupported request, a refused profile, an audited nonidentification finding and a failed computation. Failed or nonfinite output must not silently publish a partial estimate. Qualification would compare the original package against exact score and aggregation fixtures, test OOB and new-data routing, preserve warnings and refusal behavior, and check persistence and process failures. Public generated laws can then assess bias, interval coverage, widths and failed requests before an expensive bank trial. The small examples above establish the logic of that experiment, not its outcome.

Porting the wrapper or embedding its native core remains an unattractive initial route because it transfers nuisance, targeting, uncertainty and lifetime responsibilities without an observed benefit. Bayesian bcf, XBCF and flexBCF remain substantive comparators with different priors, uncertainty and restoration contracts, as developed in the preceding comparison. An original R worker is therefore the preferred prototype candidate, conditional on its environment and rights review. The measured comparison must include validation, auxiliary fits, training, scoring, serialization, human review and support costs for accepted outputs. No present evidence establishes a speed advantage, lower total cost or bank value for any route.~\citep{exec21_grf_reuse,exec21_cxx_bcf,exec21_cxx_xbcf,exec21_cxx_flexbcf}

